1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,037
This video explains what a compiler is, and the five phases that every compiler has.

2
00:00:06,487 --> 00:00:21,293
First we look at programming languages and how they are implemented. Then we follow one small program through the five phases: lexical analysis, parsing, semantic analysis, optimization and code generation.

3
00:00:21,743 --> 00:00:26,415
This course is about compiler construction. You learn two kinds of things.

4
00:00:26,865 --> 00:00:37,979
First, formal language theory. For example, regular languages and context-free languages. These give exact descriptions of what a program may look like.

5
00:00:38,429 --> 00:00:53,405
Second, the stages of a modern compiler. Lexical analysis, grammars and parsing. Type checking. Static analysis. Code optimization. Code generation. Memory management and runtime support.

6
00:00:53,855 --> 00:00:58,591
The theory tells you what is possible. The stages tell you how to build it.

7
00:00:59,041 --> 00:01:07,041
A programming language is a precise mathematical construct that lets you program a computer. It has two aspects.

8
00:01:07,491 --> 00:01:17,177
The first aspect is syntax. Syntax is the set of language constructs, for example if-else and while. It is the form of the program.

9
00:01:17,627 --> 00:01:35,205
The second aspect is semantics: what the constructs mean. Here, if x is greater than 0, y becomes 1. Else y becomes 2. Semantics assumes a model of computation, for example Turing machines or mathematical functions.

10
00:01:35,655 --> 00:01:41,927
There are three styles to define semantics: denotational, operational and axiomatic.

11
00:01:42,377 --> 00:01:55,135
Languages fall into two large groups. In a declarative language, you specify what to compute. The run-time and the compiler find out how. Examples are SQL and Matlab.

12
00:01:55,585 --> 00:02:02,987
Pure functional languages, such as Haskell, and logic-based languages, such as Prolog, are declarative.

13
00:02:03,437 --> 00:02:13,144
In an imperative language, you specify both what to compute and how to compute it. Examples are C, C plus plus, Java and Python.

14
00:02:13,594 --> 00:02:22,767
Most languages are declarative, imperative or mixed. You can add features such as object orientation to any of these groups.

15
00:02:23,217 --> 00:02:41,671
Programming language design is not easy. A language must do many things. It must let programmers make efficient code. It must help them build secure and correct programs. It must discourage bad practices, be easy to use, and automate routine tasks.

16
00:02:42,121 --> 00:02:55,177
These goals pull against each other. The course notes use the example of automatic array bounds checking. It costs the compiler engineer time and thought. The compiler runs a little longer.

17
00:02:55,627 --> 00:03:02,667
The executable program is a bit slower and a bit bigger, because it must remember the size of every array.

18
00:03:03,117 --> 00:03:05,378
But the programmer saves time and thought.

19
00:03:05,828 --> 00:03:15,172
And the end user is safe from buffer overflow attacks. Every language feature is a trade-off like this. This is a theme of the course.

20
00:03:15,622 --> 00:03:21,147
How is a language implemented? There are two major strategies, and a mix of the two.

21
00:03:21,597 --> 00:03:39,048
An interpreter runs the program as is, with little or no preprocessing. It takes the program and the input, and it gives the output. An interpreter is a virtual machine. JavaScript, TypeScript, Python and Java bytecode are mostly interpreted.

22
00:03:39,498 --> 00:03:55,327
A compiler does extensive preprocessing and offline optimization first. It makes an executable. Later, the executable runs on the input. Batch compilation systems such as gcc and clang dominate.

23
00:03:55,777 --> 00:04:08,215
The mixed strategy is just-in-time compilation, or JIT. Some environments, such as Lisp, give both: an interpreter for development and a compiler for production.

24
00:04:08,665 --> 00:04:23,193
What is a compiler? A compiler is a program that translates a program, usually in a high-level language, into a lower-level language. Here is the small program x equals 2 plus 3, from the course notes.

25
00:04:23,643 --> 00:04:38,619
First the compiler parses the string into a tree. This is the abstract syntax tree, or AST. The root is the assignment. Its left child is x. Its right child is the plus, with 2 and 3 below it.

26
00:04:39,069 --> 00:04:44,146
Then code generation turns the tree into assembly code. Four instructions.

27
00:04:44,596 --> 00:04:47,561
Run them. Move 2 into register e a x.

28
00:04:48,011 --> 00:04:50,614
Move 3 into register e b x.

29
00:04:51,064 --> 00:04:55,331
Add e b x to e a x. Now e a x holds 5.

30
00:04:55,781 --> 00:05:12,101
Push e a x. The stack holds 5, which is 2 plus 3. The output program must do the same thing as the input program. On the way, the compiler also checks the program for errors, optimizes it, and links it to code libraries.

31
00:05:12,551 --> 00:05:19,207
Some history. In 1954, IBM developed the 704 computer.

32
00:05:19,657 --> 00:05:26,505
There was a problem. Software costs were higher than hardware costs. All programming was done in assembly.

33
00:05:26,955 --> 00:05:46,283
John Backus had an idea: translate high-level code to assembly. Many people thought that this was impossible, and it had failed in other projects. The FORTRAN I project ran from 1954 to 1957. FORTRAN means formula translation.

34
00:05:46,733 --> 00:05:52,215
By 1958, more than 50 percent of all software was in FORTRAN.

35
00:05:52,665 --> 00:06:07,001
Development time was halved, and execution efficiency was only 20 percent lower. FORTRAN I was the first compiler. It led to a lot of theoretical work, and modern compilers still keep its outline.

36
00:06:07,451 --> 00:06:14,513
A compiler is a linear pipeline. Each phase makes the data for the next phase. There are five phases.

37
00:06:14,963 --> 00:06:23,069
Phase 1 is lexical analysis. The lexical analyzer reads the input program and makes a stream of tokens.

38
00:06:23,519 --> 00:06:30,773
Phase 2 is parsing. The parser reads the tokens and builds the abstract syntax tree, the AST.

39
00:06:31,223 --> 00:06:40,204
Phase 3 is semantic analysis, for example type checking. It produces an intermediate representation, or IR.

40
00:06:40,654 --> 00:06:46,862
Phase 4 is optimization. It changes the IR, here into three-address format.

41
00:06:47,312 --> 00:06:51,792
Phase 5 is code generation. It writes the output program.

42
00:06:52,242 --> 00:07:03,207
We can understand the first three phases by comparison with the way people understand a natural language, such as English. We now go through the phases one by one.

43
00:07:03,657 --> 00:07:11,231
Phase 1. When you read, the first step is to recognize words. A word is the smallest unit above letters.

44
00:07:11,681 --> 00:07:14,753
This is a sentence has four words and a period.

45
00:07:15,203 --> 00:07:29,091
The lexical analyzer does the same with program text. It divides the text into words, called tokens. Here is the line: if x equals equals y then z equals 1, else z equals 2.

46
00:07:29,541 --> 00:07:49,573
Each token has a unit, shown by colour. Keywords: if, then, else. Variables: x, y and z twice. Operators: the equals equals and two equals signs. Constants: 1 and 2. Punctuation: two semicolons. The spaces are white space. White space is also a unit.

47
00:07:50,023 --> 00:07:58,876
So the lexer finds 14 tokens and 11 white space characters. Video 2 shows exactly how the lexer finds them.

48
00:07:59,326 --> 00:08:10,483
Phase 2. When the words are known, the next step is to understand the structure of the sentence. Parsing is diagramming sentences, and the diagram is a tree.

49
00:08:10,933 --> 00:08:20,960
Here is the sentence: This line is a long sentence. First, each word gets its category. Article, noun, verb, article, adjective, noun.

50
00:08:21,410 --> 00:08:27,106
Then the words are grouped. This line is the subject. A long sentence is the object.

51
00:08:27,556 --> 00:08:32,783
Subject, verb and object together make the sentence. That is the root of the tree.

52
00:08:33,233 --> 00:08:44,411
Parsing a program is similar. Take the same line: if x equals equals y then z equals 1, else z equals 2. The leaves of the tree are tokens.

53
00:08:44,861 --> 00:08:53,032
x equals equals y is a relation. z equals 1 is an assignment. z equals 2 is another assignment.

54
00:08:53,482 --> 00:09:00,394
The relation is the predicate. The first assignment is the then statement. The second is the else statement.

55
00:09:00,844 --> 00:09:15,500
All three together make one if-then-else statement, the root. Only 7 of the 14 tokens are leaves. The keywords, the equals signs and the semicolons are not leaves. They tell the parser the shape of the tree.

56
00:09:15,950 --> 00:09:24,803
Phase 3. When the structure is known, we try to understand the meaning. Take the sentence: I saw a man on a hill with a telescope.

57
00:09:25,253 --> 00:09:32,763
It looks simple. But it has several meanings. Reading 1: there is a man on a hill, and I watch him with my telescope.

58
00:09:33,213 --> 00:09:37,266
Reading 2: there is a man on a hill, and he has a telescope.

59
00:09:37,716 --> 00:09:42,495
Reading 3: the man is on a hill, and the hill has a telescope on it.

60
00:09:42,945 --> 00:09:51,094
Reading 4: I am on a hill, and I saw a man with my telescope. The word saw also has more than one meaning.

61
00:09:51,544 --> 00:10:00,760
Understanding the meaning of programs automatically is too hard. So compilers do a limited analysis to catch inconsistencies.

62
00:10:01,210 --> 00:10:12,197
English has another problem. Jack said Jack left his assignment at home. How many Jacks are there? Is Jack talking about himself? Which one left the assignment?

63
00:10:12,647 --> 00:10:29,137
Programming languages define strict rules to avoid this kind of ambiguity. These are variable binding rules, or scoping rules. In this C plus plus code, there are two variables called Jack. The second one is in an inner scope.

64
00:10:29,587 --> 00:10:39,443
The output statement is in the inner scope. The rule says: use the nearest definition. So it uses the inner Jack. The outer Jack is hidden.

65
00:10:39,893 --> 00:10:44,117
The program prints 4. We compiled and ran this code to check it.

66
00:10:44,567 --> 00:10:54,935
Compilers perform many semantic checks besides variable binding. One of them is type checking. Take the sentence: Jack left her homework at home.

67
00:10:55,385 --> 00:11:02,319
There is a type mismatch between her and Jack. We know they are different people. Presumably Jack is male.

68
00:11:02,769 --> 00:11:17,382
In a program, Jack is declared as an int. Then the program assigns a string to it. The types do not match. The C plus plus compiler reports an error and rejects the program. We checked this with the compiler.

69
00:11:17,832 --> 00:11:32,339
Phase 4 is optimization. English has no strong counterpart. It is like editing. The compiler changes the program automatically, so that it runs faster, or uses less memory or less power.

70
00:11:32,789 --> 00:11:40,383
Here is a small function. The slide example: x equals y times 0 is the same as x equals 0.

71
00:11:40,833 --> 00:11:50,156
So the test x equals equals 1 is never true. The code inside it never executes. That is dead code, and the compiler removes it.

72
00:11:50,606 --> 00:11:59,267
The result returns 2 directly. We compiled this function with optimization on. The output has no test and returns 2.

73
00:11:59,717 --> 00:12:11,579
There are other optimizations: register allocation, cache-aware optimizations, and special-purpose instructions, for example for graphics or the fast Fourier transform.

74
00:12:12,029 --> 00:12:23,037
Can a correct optimization introduce a security problem? The answer is yes. Optimizations preserve defined behaviour, not the intent of the programmer.

75
00:12:23,487 --> 00:12:30,121
This function reads star p first. If p is NULL, that read is undefined behaviour in C.

76
00:12:30,571 --> 00:12:41,558
Undefined behaviour gives the optimizer surprising freedom. It may assume that undefined behaviour never happens. So it assumes that p is not NULL.

77
00:12:42,008 --> 00:12:58,200
Then the NULL test can never be true, and the optimizer removes it. We checked this. Clang with optimization level 2 removes the test. With optimization off, the test stays. The programmer wanted a safety check, and it is gone.

78
00:12:58,650 --> 00:13:04,325
Phase 5 is code generation. It translates the program into another language.

79
00:13:04,775 --> 00:13:09,788
It usually produces assembly code, for a special-purpose instruction set.

80
00:13:10,238 --> 00:13:16,595
A good compiler is retargetable: the same compiler can generate code for different hardware.

81
00:13:17,045 --> 00:13:25,515
A compiler does a series of translations between intermediate forms. The first is the source. The last is assembly.

82
00:13:25,965 --> 00:13:42,989
All the forms between them are intermediate languages, internal to the compiler. Sometimes there is only one, called the IR. The intermediate languages are ordered by level of abstraction. The source is highest and assembly is lowest.

83
00:13:43,439 --> 00:13:46,788
Higher levels keep the high-level meaning of the program.

84
00:13:47,238 --> 00:13:59,334
Lower levels expose features that higher levels hide, such as registers and memory layout. This enables new optimizations. But lower levels hide the high-level meaning.

85
00:13:59,784 --> 00:14:12,883
To design an IR that exposes the hardware and also keeps the structure is very hard. The slides show MLIR, a compiler framework with multiple levels of IR.

86
00:14:13,333 --> 00:14:27,199
The overall structure of almost every compiler follows this outline. But the proportions have changed since FORTRAN. In early compilers, lexing and parsing were the most complex and expensive parts.

87
00:14:27,649 --> 00:14:38,679
Today, optimization dominates all the other phases. Lexing and parsing are cheap. The bar widths only show this idea. They are not measured values.

88
00:14:39,129 --> 00:14:44,590
Lexing and parsing are cheap today because the theory in this course made them routine.

89
00:14:45,040 --> 00:14:50,416
What is the core of a compiler? It depends on the language and on the application.

90
00:14:50,866 --> 00:15:05,010
For C, C plus plus and other low-level imperative languages, the core is optimization and program analysis: alias analysis, global and link-time optimization, and bug detection.

91
00:15:05,460 --> 00:15:17,492
For Java, JavaScript and other interpreted languages, the core is just-in-time compilation, garbage collection, polymorphism and efficient object orientation.

92
00:15:17,942 --> 00:15:25,387
For TensorFlow and other machine learning languages, the core is the IR, scheduling and parallelization.

93
00:15:25,837 --> 00:15:37,357
ECE 351 compiles a hardware description language. Its core is parsing, optimization, code generation and object-oriented design.

94
00:15:37,807 --> 00:15:46,063
The course notes show the stages of a typical C compiler. The boxes are the kinds of data. The arrows are the stages.

95
00:15:46,513 --> 00:16:03,431
ECE 351 focuses on the stages in orange. Lexing chunks the input into a list of tokens, and usually removes white space. Parsing builds the AST from the tokens. Code generation makes the output.

96
00:16:03,881 --> 00:16:14,249
Optimization changes the intermediate language into a better intermediate language. That is also an ECE 351 focus.

97
00:16:14,699 --> 00:16:31,936
Now an exercise. Divide this line into tokens: while x not equals 0 do x equals x minus 1, semicolon. Give the unit of each token: keyword, variable, operator, constant or punctuation. Then count them.

98
00:16:32,386 --> 00:16:34,029
Pause the video and try it.

99
00:16:34,479 --> 00:16:45,593
Read from left to right. while is a keyword. x is a variable. Not equals is one operator. It is two characters, but one token. 0 is a constant.

100
00:16:46,043 --> 00:16:51,270
do is a keyword. Then x, equals, x, minus, 1, and the semicolon.

101
00:16:51,720 --> 00:17:05,565
In total: 2 keywords, 3 variables, 3 operators, 2 constants and 1 punctuation mark. That is 11 tokens. The 9 spaces are white space. The lexer usually removes them.

102
00:17:06,015 --> 00:17:12,458
To summarize. A language has syntax and semantics. Every design choice is a trade-off.

103
00:17:12,908 --> 00:17:21,057
Interpreters run programs as is. Compilers translate them first. Just-in-time compilation mixes both.

104
00:17:21,507 --> 00:17:30,147
A compiler has five phases: lexical analysis, parsing, semantic analysis, optimization and code generation.

105
00:17:30,597 --> 00:17:38,341
Between the phases flow tokens, the AST, the IR, three-address format, and finally the output program.

106
00:17:38,791 --> 00:17:59,698
Optimizations preserve defined behaviour, not intent. Today optimization dominates, and lexing and parsing are cheap. Many open problems remain, for example parallel code generation, power optimization and better type systems. Next video: lexical analysis in detail.
